              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Conversely, a man's own children would inherit from their own mjomba — from their mother's brother. A father's property would bypass his children entirely and flow to his sister's line. This created a system of cross-generational investment in which every man had a stake in two sets of children: his own (whom he raised) and his sister's (who would inherit from him). The father-child bond was one of daily care and affection. The mjomba-nephew bond was one of structural obligation and material inheritance."
