              "text": "The kaya forests of the Digo and the broader Mijikenda peoples are among the most remarkable examples on earth of spiritual practice producing ecological outcomes. For centuries, these sacred groves — the fortified hilltop settlements established after the migration from Singwaya — have been protected by a system of taboos enforced through spiritual sanctions. No one may cut a living tree in the kaya. No livestock may graze within its boundaries. Entry is restricted to those with legitimate purpose, and even then, protocols of respect must be observed. Violators historically faced fines of livestock, which were sacrificed to appease the ancestral spirits believed to inhabit the forest."
