              "text": "Kwale County, the heartland of the Digo people, contains within its 8,270 square kilometres a degree of ecological diversity that defies its modest size. The county stretches from the Indian Ocean shoreline westward into the continental interior, and as it does, it passes through four distinct topographical zones — each with its own elevation, soil type, vegetation, rainfall pattern, and economic logic. These zones are not administrative abstractions. They are lived realities that determine what a family can grow, how much water they can access, whether they fish or farm or herd, and how they understand their relationship to the land. To know which zone a Digo family inhabits is to know much about how they live."
