              "text": "But trade is not the only traffic at Lunga Lunga. The crossing carries a constant flow of family visits — grandparents going to see grandchildren, siblings attending each other's weddings, extended families gathering for funerals. Cross-border marriages remain common among the Digo, and these unions create family networks that require regular border crossings to maintain. A wedding on the Tanzanian side will draw guests from Kwale. A funeral in Msambweni will bring mourners from Mkinga. The border complicates these journeys with paperwork and waiting times, but it does not prevent them. Family obligations, in Digo culture, are not optional, and no customs post can override the duty to attend when kinship calls."
